1. Java Objects (including arrays), custom objects are passed by reference which means that when you pass an object to
a method, the method gets a reference (or pointer) to that object, not a copy of the object itself.
So, when you modify the object's state (it's properties or values) inside the method, those changes will be reflected
in the original object from the calling code because both the method and the calling code are referencing
the same object in memory.
Strings and Wrapper classes are immutable so even if they are object as passed by reference , the change is not
reflected in the calling part

Arrays and Objects are passed by reference in java
This means that changes made to the array or object in the method will affect the original
array or object in the calling code.
Primitive Data Types (like int, double etc) are passed by value
When you pass a primitive to a method the method gets a copy of the value, so changes inside the method
do not affect the original value in the calling code.

public class Example {
    public static void modifyArray(int[] array) {
        array[0] = 100; // Modifying the array element
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] numbers = {1, 2, 3};
        modifyArray(numbers); // Passing array to the method
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(numbers)); // Output: [100, 2, 3]
    }
}


public class Example {
    public static void modifyValue(int value) {
        value = 100; // Modifying the value (won't affect the original)
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int num = 5;
        modifyValue(num); // Passing primitive to the method
        System.out.println(num); // Output: 5 (no change)
    }
}
